elative risk 2.5 to 7 times."]},{"id":"rt-c11","title":"Diagnostic work-up of UTUC","topic":"UTUC diagnosis","priority":"High","marks":8,"subtopics":["utuc-pathology-diagnosis"],"scenario":"An upper tract urothelial lesion is suspected after hematuria and abnormal imaging.","questions":["What lower urinary tract examination should always be performed?","What endoscopic procedure with biopsy is a key diagnostic approach?","How can accurate cytology be obtained?"],"answer":["Cystoscopy should always be performed.","Flexible ureteroscopy with biopsy.","Urine collection or washing of the ureter using a ureteral catheter can provide the most accurate cytologic results."]},{"id":"rt-c12","title":"Definitive operation for UTUC","topic":"UTUC treatment","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["utuc-treatment"],"scenario":"A patient has an upper tract urothelial carcinoma on the affected side.","questions":["State the operation described in the lecture.","Which bladder component is removed?"],"answer":["Nephroureterectomy.","Removal of a cuff of bladder around the ureteral orifice of the affected side."]}],"coreShorts":[{"id":"rt-s01","q":"Classify renal masses by biological category.","a":["Malignant.","Benign.","Inflammatory."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-s02","q":"Give examples of benign renal masses.","a":["Renal cyst.","Angiomyolipoma.","Oncocytoma."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-s03","q":"Give examples of malignant renal masses.","a":["Renal cell carcinoma.","Wilms tumor.","Urothelial carcinoma."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-s04","q":"Give examples of inflammatory renal masses.","a":["Abscess.","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis.","Tuberculosis."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-s05","q":"Classify renal masses by radiographic appearance.","a":["Simple cystic.","Complex cystic.","Solid."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-s06","q":"State the peak age and sex ratio of RCC.","a":["Peak incidence: 60-70 years.","Male-to-female ratio: 3:2."],"topic":"RCC epidemiology","priority":"High","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-s07","q":"List the lifestyle-related etiological factors for RCC.","a":["Smoking.","Obesity.","Hypertension."],"topic":"RCC risk factors","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-s08","q":"Describe the VHL association with RCC.","a":["Von Hippel-Lindau disease is autosomal dominant.","It is due to mutation of the VHL gene on chromosome 3p."],"topic":"RCC genetics","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-s09","q":"From which cells are RCCs derived?","a":["Renal tubular epithelial cells; RCCs are adenocarcinomas."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"High","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s10","q":"Describe the gross outline and capsule of RCC.","a":["Usually round to ovoid.","Circumscribed by a pseudocapsule of compressed parenchyma and fibrous tissue, not a true histologic capsule."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s11","q":"Why is RCC yellow on gross examination?","a":["Because of high fat content."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s12","q":"When is multicentricity common in RCC?","a":["In familial RCC."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s13","q":"List the histologic subtypes of RCC with the percentages stated.","a":["Clear cell: 70%-80%.","Papillary: 10%-15%.","Chromophobe: 4%-5%.","Unclassified: 1%-5%."],"topic":"RCC histology","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s14","q":"Which RCC subtype has poor prognosis?","a":["Collecting duct carcinoma."],"topic":"RCC histology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s15","q":"Which RCC subtype is associated with sickle cell trait?","a":["Renal medullary carcinoma."],"topic":"RCC histology","priority":"High","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s16","q":"State the classic clinical triad of RCC.","a":["Flank pain.","Painless gross hematuria.","Palpable abdominal mass."],"topic":"RCC clinical diagnosis","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical"]},{"id":"rt-s17","q":"List physical examination findings in RCC.","a":["Flank or abdominal mass.","Left supraclavicular lymph node (Virchow gland).","Non-reducible varicocele due to testicular vein obstruction."],"topic":"RCC examination","priority":"Core","marks":4,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical"]},{"id":"rt-s18","q":"List the paraneoplastic and constitutional manifestations stated for RCC.","a":["Hypertension.","Hypercalcemia.","Polycythemia.","Non-metastatic hepatic dysfunction.","Fever and cachexia."],"topic":"RCC paraneoplastic syndromes","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical"]},{"id":"rt-s19","q":"List the laboratory investigations for RCC.","a":["Urinalysis for hematuria.","CBC for anemia or polycythemia.","Creatinine.","Liver function tests."],"topic":"RCC investigations","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["rcc-investigations"]},{"id":"rt-s20","q":"Compare US, contrast CT and MRI in RCC.","a":["US may show a solid or partially cystic mass.","Contrast CT is used for diagnosis and staging.","MRI is useful for diagnosis and staging without ionizing radiation."],"topic":"RCC imaging","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["rcc-investigations"]},{"id":"rt-s21","q":"State the surgical treatment according to renal tumor size.","a":["Partial nephrectomy for a small renal tumor.","Radical nephrectomy for a larger tumor."],"topic":"RCC treatment","priority":"High","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-treatment"]},{"id":"rt-s22","q":"Give the core pathology facts of Wilms tumor.","a":["Most common childhood abdominal malignancy.","Arises from embryonic nephrogenic tissue.","Typical age stated: 4 years."],"topic":"Wilms pathology","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["wilms-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s23","q":"Describe the gross and microscopic classification of Wilms tumor.","a":["Gross: large, multilobulated and gray.","Microscopy: favorable or unfavorable histology."],"topic":"Wilms pathology","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["wilms-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-s24","q":"List the clinical findings of Wilms tumor.","a":["Abdominal mass.","Abdominal pain and distention.","Anorexia, nausea and vomiting.","Hematuria.","Hypertension due to renin production."],"topic":"Wilms presentation","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["wilms-clinical"]},{"id":"rt-s25","q":"List the differential diagnosis of a flank mass in a child.","a":["Hydronephrosis.","Cystic kidney diseases.","Neuroblastoma.","Sarcomas."],"topic":"Wilms differential","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["wilms-clinical"]},{"id":"rt-s26","q":"State the investigations for Wilms tumor.","a":["Urinalysis and CBC.","Ultrasound as the primary investigation.","Abdominal CT.","MRI because it avoids ionizing radiation."],"topic":"Wilms investigations","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["wilms-investigations"]},{